my father took his own life  he had terminal cancer  and he took himself off without telling  the family so as not to implicate them  and he died alone  in his car  and i felt  very angry that he had to do that  it's a controversial debate  assisted dying has divided opinion for  years  i've had patients who i thought had a  prognosis of months or even weeks  they've been alive years later  but what exactly  is it and could it really become legal  in the uk  assisted dying is the legal right for  terminally ill mentally competent people  to choose to end their lives different  countries have different rules about who  can choose to die this way well  i feel very strongly and passionately  about it right now it's illegal in the  uk  three years ago i got diagnosed with an  incurable illness a bone marrow cancer  and i thought well what do i want to do  and it was pretty clear to me that i  wanted to  take my own life at a time when i wanted  to  when pain was becoming intolerable it's  not something i want to do i'm certainly  not suicidal and i want to live for as  long as possible but i don't want to go  through the last throws and in the  indignity  of of dying  possibly with incontinence  in terrible pain racked with terrible  pain  previous proposals to change the law  have said that two doctors should assess  each request the person applying would  then be given drugs to take to help them  die when they feel ready  it's a popular idea with the public  a 2021 survey found that 73 of the  general population supported so-called  doctor-assisted suicide for terminally  ill patients 53 percent of people also  supported allowing doctors to help  people suffering from painful incurable  but not terminal illnesses to die  despite that support repeated attempts  in the courts and in parliament to  legalize it have all failed  order order  the ice to the right 118 the notes to  the left 330.  the law as it stands is antiquated  my father killed himself with carbon  monoxide poisoning and the car caught  fire the first thing i knew about it was  the police ringing up and saying we've  found your father in a burnt car i had  to go and identify his body  and that was the most horrendous  experience  i think just the anguish that he must  have gone through he was a very brave  man  but i felt so strongly that he shouldn't  have had to do that because we all  deserve  i think to have a dignified death  some campaigners are fiercely against  the idea they worry it could be open to  abuse and force people to think about  ending their lives when they may not  really want to  i think for the public my concern is  that people don't understand what they  can do already in law they can refuse  life prolonging treatment they can  refuse any treatment they must be looked  after clinicians have a duty of care to  look after them as they die of their  disease but the biggest problem is that  we know that doctors and nurses are not  good at picking up pressure on people we  know from oregon that 53 percent of  people said that fear of being a burden  or feeling that they are a burden was  one of their concerns about end of life  pain came way down the list so these are  social issues that are driving people to  feel that they need to end their lives  early  anyone in switzerland provided they  don't do it for selfish reasons and they  have the help of a doctor can legally  end their own life  other countries like spain canada and  the state of oregon in the u.s also  allow assisted dying but switzerland is  notable because it allows non-residents  to travel there to die  and people from the uk go there quite a  lot  since 1998 the swiss non-profit charity  dignitas has helped nearly 500 britons  take their own life the second highest  of any country in the world on average  20 people travel there from the uk every  year and it's legal for britons to go  but it's also very expensive even  without travel dignitas says it can cost  between six to eight thousand pounds  depending on funeral arrangements to end  your life this way so not everyone can  afford to go  assisted dying is about to be debated in  the house of lords for the first time in  six years baroness meech's plan includes  more safeguards than previous  suggestions it says that two doctors and  a judge have to sign off on anyone  wanting help to die we do have a clear  majority  in in the house of lords which you know  we just about had i think when it was  last tested six years ago but it's much  clearer today  the medical community is also changing  its stance in september 2021 the british  medical association which has  historically opposed a change to the law  adopted a neutral position that means  they won't oppose or support a change to  assisted dying law  if the bill goes through the house of  lords and campaigners think they have  enough support for that to happen then  it would be up to mps to debate and  decide and some think there's enough  support there for it to pass for the  first time ever  but while there is support across the  country some are still wary  we know that there are deficits in care  hospices are funded out of charitable  money they're not even nhs funded yet  and yet we're saying here we are we'll  have doctors supply lethal drugs to  patients against some criteria that you  can't  assess objectively and properly  scotland is already ahead of westminster  there could be a vote in the scottish  parliament on assisted dying within just  18 months and campaigners think the  plans will get the go-ahead with strict  safeguards i think palliative care as  you say has moved on hugely but if  baroness meecher's bill becomes law  there are so many safeguards in place it  would only cover people who were in this  last six months of their lives and were  suffering from a terminal illness it's  our autonomy our individual right  [Music]  you
 

